<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Djem</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thysdrus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El Djem is a town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> on the site of the Roman city of Thysdrus, which grew rich on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>. In 238 a revolt there proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-i">Gordian I</a> emperor. Its great amphitheatre, built in the 3rd century for some 35,000 spectators, is one of the largest in the Roman world and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-djem.jpg" alt="El Djem" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Amphitheatre_of_El_Jem,_built_around_AD_238_in_Thysdrus_in_Africa_Proconsularis,_the_estimated_capacity_is_35,000,_Tunisia_-_52717762494.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
